# The hashish bar's block faces — a pressed slab of dry sift lying on the floor.
#
# PLACEHOLDER art. Warlon Mhite may redraw it; that is what this file is for.
#
# ## Why it looks nothing like item/hashish even though it is the same material
#
# Items are high contrast and blocks are not: vanilla items span a median 164 luminance steps
# against a block's 76. item/hashish is a single small sprite that has to read at 16 px in a
# hotbar, so it runs its whole ramp from near-black to a bright sheen. These are surfaces seen
# in the world at whatever size the player is standing at, so the ramp is deliberately narrow
# and the sheen is a suggestion rather than a highlight.
#
# ## Why both faces tile
#
# The block model is a cuboid that shrinks along one axis as the bar is cut, and its faces take
# auto-generated UVs — so each cut state samples a *different sub-region* of these 16x16 files.
# A texture with a composed centre would slide about as the bar shrank. A uniform, tileable
# pressed surface samples the same at every length, which is what makes five models out of two
# textures possible at all.
#
# Palette is the hash brown from item/hashish and the strain entry's own 0x6B4A2F, pulled
# together and flattened for block contrast.

@palette resin
. = none
1 = 2A1D0E      the deepest pits in the press
2 = 39280F      shadow
3 = 47311A      the body of the surface, and the commonest tone
4 = 553C21      lit body
5 = 6B4A2F      the strain colour itself — the tone the whole family is keyed to
6 = 7A5738      pressed sheen, sparse and scattered

# Seen from above: a pressed cake, dense and slightly uneven, with the sheen broken up rather
# than gradiented. A straight corner-to-corner fade is pancake shading and the classic tell.
@texture block/hashish_bar_top 16x16 type=block tiling=true
3342333334234333
2633233633233333
4333333333333334
3335333433332443
